    <h1 class="display mt-4 text-4xl text-[var(--color-ink)]">Windows × Ghidra wiring</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-4 !max-w-none">Bring-your-own Ghidra on Windows for read-oriented native PE/DLL work — env vars, doctor, and cold-start reality.</p>
    <h2>Bring your own installs</h2>
    <p>REA does not download Ghidra or a JDK. Install a supported Ghidra build and a 64-bit JDK outside REA, then export absolute paths before setup:</p>
    <pre><code>setx GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR C:\path\to\ghidra_PUBLIC
setx JAVA_HOME C:\path\to\jdk-21
setx REA_ANALYSIS_PROVIDER ghidra</code></pre>
    <p>Open a new shell so the variables are visible, then:</p>
    <pre><code>npx -y rea-agents@latest doctor --provider ghidra --json
npx rea-agents@latest setup</code></pre>
    <h2>What Windows P0 is (and is not)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Oriented toward approved native x86-64 PE applications and related DLL inspection via headless Ghidra.</li>
      <li>Read-oriented tool surface: inventory, decompilation, references — not a GUI puppet that mutates your Ghidra project arbitrarily.</li>
      <li>Historical docs once disagreed about Windows native authority gates; if doctor says the provider is disabled, upgrade and re-read current upstream Windows Ghidra notes before assuming the product lied.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Cold start &amp; timeouts</h2>
    <p>First headless launch can exceed short default timeouts (community reports of multi-minute JVM/Ghidra bring-up). If the first query fails with a provider timeout while later retries work, lengthen startup patience (see large-binary guide) rather than thrashing install paths.</p>
    <h2>Authorized targets only</h2>
    <p>Point Ghidra+REA at binaries you own, build, or have written permission to analyze.</p>

